[{:db/id 17592186229158 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-1634e1ab-c584-5b67-9e78-d7bac12ef42f" "question-a5501ecd-a534-5143-a2fa-d60083b2450a" "question-2a221524-8030-56ce-9632-c32e19b2687d"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186228903 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true when testing the stability of Euler's method on the test equation $y' = -\\lambda y$ with $y = y(x), \\, y(0) = 8, \\ x \\geq 0, \\, \\lambda = 6,$ and step size $\\Delta x?$  \n\nI. The update formula can be written as $y_{n+1} = \\left(1 - 6\\Delta x\\right)^n y_n.$\n\nII. The method is stable for $|1 - 6\\Delta x| < 1.$\n\nIII. The method is stable for step size $\\Delta x = 0.1.$\n\nIV. The method is **unstable** for step size $\\Delta x = 0.2.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186229159 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-1f49a9a1-5ba0-5f85-aec8-d5a800fd601f" "question-eb08f354-4c44-52a2-9c76-c85c4970a5f5" "question-28574ce8-fd0a-501f-b680-ed91403e93fc"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186228905 :question/problem "For which of the following step sizes is Euler's Method stable, given that $\\lambda = 1$ is the parameter of the test equation $y' = -\\lambda y$ with $y = y(x), y(0) = 1, x \\geq 0,$ and step size $\\Delta x?$\n\nI. $\\Delta x = 0.5$\n\nII. $\\Delta x = 0.9$\n\nIII. $\\Delta x = 1.2$\n\nIV. $\\Delta x = 1.8$\n\n*Hint: The stability function for Euler's method is given by* $R(\\lambda\\Delta x) = 1 - \\lambda \\Delta x.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186229160 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-acf9ba03-3b41-5ee5-9008-523784ef9cff" "question-639c33be-3d9f-5ed3-823e-9a34ba608c41" "question-ad84f498-bd85-5632-acda-fbc8df689a1e"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186228904 :question/problem "For the initial value problem $y' = f(x,y), y(x_0) = y_0,$ the modified Euler method can be expressed as the update formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{n}^{(p)} & =f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ {y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}^{(p)}))\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true when testing the stability of the modified Euler method on the test equation $y'=-\\lambda y$ with $y = y(x), y(0) = 1, x \\geq 0, \\lambda = 12,$ and step size $\\Delta x?$  \n\nI. The stability function is $R(12\\Delta x) = 1 - 12\\Delta x + 72(\\Delta x)^2.$\n\nII. The stability region is $\\left|72\\left(\\Delta x - \\dfrac16\\right)^2\\right| < 1.$\n\nIII. The method is absolutely stable.\n\nIV. The method is stable for step size $\\Delta x = 0.15.$\n\n*Hint:* The stability function $R$ is given by $y_{n+1} = R(\\lambda\\Delta x)y_n.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186229161 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-b1d62ba0-0114-5483-9f12-c0fecd4e79bf" "question-cf3c4ec0-335d-5e9d-91d7-9dd1f9e287ee"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186228906 :question/problem "For which of the following values is the Runge–Kutta Method (RK4) stable, given that $\\lambda$ is the parameter of the test equation?\n\nI. $\\lambda\\Delta x = 0.6$\n\nII. $\\lambda\\Delta x = 1.4$\n\nIII. $\\lambda\\Delta x = 2.8$\n\nIV. $\\lambda\\Delta x = 3.3$\n\n*Hint: The stability function $R$ of the RK4 method is given by*\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda\\Delta x)\n= 1 - \\lambda\\Delta x\n  + \\dfrac{(\\lambda\\Delta x)^2}{2}\n  - \\dfrac{(\\lambda\\Delta x)^3}{6}\n  + \\dfrac{(\\lambda\\Delta x)^4}{24}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186229652 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true when testing the stability of Euler's method on the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=1,x\\ge 0,\\lambda =4,$ and step size $Δx?$  \n\nI. The update formula can be written as ${y}_{n+1}=(1-4Δx)^{n}{y}_{n}.$\n\nII. The method is stable for $|1-4Δx|<1.$\n\nIII. The method is stable for step size $Δx=0.2.$\n\nIV. The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx=0.3.$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of Euler's Method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-4y,\\,y(0)=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, for an initial value problem ${y}^{′}=f(x,y),y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},$ Euler's method is given by the update formula\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx.\n$$\n\nNow, let's check our statements:\n\n- Applying Euler's method to the test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+(-4{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}-4{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(1-4Δx){y}_{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, statement I is false.\n\n- For stability, we require ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty .$ Here, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n} & =(1-4Δx){y}_{n-1} \\\\  & =(1-4Δx)((1-4Δx){y}_{n-2}) \\\\  & =(1-4Δx)^{2}{y}_{n-2} \\\\  & \\,\\,⋮\\,\\,⋮ \\\\  & =(1-4Δx)^{n}{y}_{0} \\\\  & =(1-4Δx)^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${y}_{n}$ converges to $0$ as $n\\to \\infty $ if and only if $|1-4Δx|<1,$ as stated. Hence, statement II is true.\n\n- Expanding the inequality $|1-4Δx|<1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & <1-4Δx<1 \\\\ -2 & <-4Δx<0 \\\\ 0 & <4Δx<2 \\\\ 0 & <Δx<0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine stability for the given step sizes:\n\n- Since $Δx=0.2$ satisfies the above inequality, Euler's method is stable for this value. Hence, statement III is true.\n\n- Since $Δx=0.3$ also satisfies the above inequality, Euler's method is stable for this value. Hence, statement IV is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186228112 :answer-field/correct 17592186228117 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-1634e1ab-c584-5b67-9e78-d7bac12ef42f" :question/id #uuid "1634e1ab-c584-5b67-9e78-d7bac12ef42f" :question/math-academy-id "q-333332" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true when testing the stability of Euler's method on the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=2,x\\ge 0,\\lambda =4,$ and step size $Δx?$  \n\nI. The update formula can be written as ${y}_{n+1}=(1-4Δx)^{n}{y}_{n}.$\n\nII. The method is stable for $|1-4Δx|<1.$\n\nIII. The method is stable for step size $Δx=0.3.$\n\nIV. The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx=0.9.$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method ${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$ is stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=2,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of Euler's Method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-4y,\\,y(0)=2.\n$$\n\nFirst, for an initial value problem ${y}^{′}=f(x,y),y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},$ Euler's method is given by the update formula\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx.\n$$\n\nNow, let's check our statements:\n\n- Applying Euler's method to the test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+(-4{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}-4{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(1-4Δx){y}_{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, statement I is false.\n\n- For stability, we require ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty .$ Here, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n} & =(1-4Δx){y}_{n-1} \\\\  & =(1-4Δx)((1-4Δx){y}_{n-2}) \\\\  & =(1-4Δx)^{2}{y}_{n-2} \\\\  & \\,\\,⋮\\,\\,⋮ \\\\  & =(1-4Δx)^{n}{y}_{0} \\\\  & =2(1-4Δx)^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${y}_{n}$ converges to $0$ as $n\\to \\infty $ if and only if $|1-4Δx|<1,$ as stated. Hence, statement II is true.\n\n- Expanding the inequality $|1-4Δx|<1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & <1-4Δx<1 \\\\ -2 & <-4Δx<0 \\\\ 0 & <4Δx<2 \\\\ 0 & <Δx<\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine stability for the given step sizes:\n\n- Since $Δx=0.3$ satisfies the above inequality, Euler's method is stable for this value. Hence, statement III is true.\n\n- Since $Δx=0.9$ does not satisfy the above inequality, Euler's method is unstable for this value. Hence, statement IV is true.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II, III, and IV only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-30b2b430-328b-5dcf-bd4a-0b17e7f6fbdf"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-30b2b430-328b-5dcf-bd4a-0b17e7f6fbdf" :answer-field/id #uuid "30b2b430-328b-5dcf-bd4a-0b17e7f6fbdf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3591a278-80f8-5cee-9c15-1158b534c901" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ffe8be3b-1eb6-5c8c-beca-600ef6e03b57" "answer-b6dcc13c-6b5b-57d7-931c-6ac2ca139b38" "answer-4679d117-60f3-5840-9334-83da2fcb078e" "answer-3591a278-80f8-5cee-9c15-1158b534c901" "answer-ea61377a-dba0-5735-a1d1-84c13949fb14"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ffe8be3b-1eb6-5c8c-beca-600ef6e03b57" :answer/id #uuid "ffe8be3b-1eb6-5c8c-beca-600ef6e03b57" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b6dcc13c-6b5b-57d7-931c-6ac2ca139b38" :answer/id #uuid "b6dcc13c-6b5b-57d7-931c-6ac2ca139b38" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4679d117-60f3-5840-9334-83da2fcb078e" :answer/id #uuid "4679d117-60f3-5840-9334-83da2fcb078e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3591a278-80f8-5cee-9c15-1158b534c901" :answer/id #uuid "3591a278-80f8-5cee-9c15-1158b534c901" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II, III, and IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ea61377a-dba0-5735-a1d1-84c13949fb14" :answer/id #uuid "ea61377a-dba0-5735-a1d1-84c13949fb14" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III and IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a5501ecd-a534-5143-a2fa-d60083b2450a" :question/id #uuid "a5501ecd-a534-5143-a2fa-d60083b2450a" :question/math-academy-id "q-333348" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true when testing the stability of Euler's method on the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),\\,y(0)=\\frac{7}{3},\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda =\\frac{11}{5},$ and step size $Δx?$  \n\nI. The update formula can be written as ${y}_{n+1}={(1-\\frac{11}{5}Δx)}^{n}{y}_{n}.$\n\nII. The method is stable for $|1-\\frac{11}{5}Δx|<1.$\n\nIII. The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx=0.3.$\n\nIV. The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx=1.$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method ${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$ is stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=\\frac{7}{3},\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of Euler's Method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\frac{11}{5}y,\\,y(0)=\\frac{7}{3}.\n$$\n\nFirst, for an initial value problem ${y}^{′}=f(x,y),y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},$ Euler's method is given by the update formula\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx.\n$$\n\nNow, let's check our statements:\n\n- Applying Euler's method to the test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+(-\\frac{11}{5}{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}-\\frac{11}{5}{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(1-\\frac{11}{5}Δx){y}_{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, statement I is false.\n\n- For stability, we require ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty .$ Here, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n} & =(1-\\frac{11}{5}Δx){y}_{n-1} \\\\  & =(1-\\frac{11}{5}Δx)((1-\\frac{11}{5}Δx){y}_{n-2}) \\\\  & ={(1-\\frac{11}{5}Δx)}^{2}{y}_{n-2} \\\\  & \\,\\,⋮\\,\\,⋮ \\\\  & ={(1-\\frac{11}{5}Δx)}^{n}{y}_{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3}{(1-\\frac{11}{5}Δx)}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${y}_{n}$ converges to $0$ as $n\\to \\infty $ if and only if $|1-\\frac{11}{5}Δx|<1,$ as stated. Hence, statement II is true.\n\n- Expanding the inequality $|1-\\frac{11}{5}Δx|<1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & <1-\\frac{11}{5}Δx<1 \\\\ -2 & <-\\frac{11}{5}Δx<0 \\\\ 0 & <\\frac{11}{5}Δx<2 \\\\ 0 & <Δx<\\frac{10}{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine stability for the given step sizes:\n\n- Since $Δx=0.3$ satisfies the above inequality, Euler's method is stable for this value. Hence, statement III is false.\n\n- Since $Δx=1$ does not satisfy the above inequality, Euler's method is unstable for this value. Hence, statement IV is true.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II and IV only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-dcc854e9-e298-54b3-acbf-549aa127409d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-dcc854e9-e298-54b3-acbf-549aa127409d" :answer-field/id #uuid "dcc854e9-e298-54b3-acbf-549aa127409d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6291fa02-aa1b-5b93-a9bb-731989a0e370" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e80a29ed-94a2-591f-9412-59e8778e3657" "answer-4ca3c1c9-556c-5824-8f78-bd156ab7ec4c" "answer-940a3ea8-8fb7-5661-9e30-87d5a6990359" "answer-6291fa02-aa1b-5b93-a9bb-731989a0e370" "answer-23762144-a04a-50eb-ae41-7d91386b34ba"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e80a29ed-94a2-591f-9412-59e8778e3657" :answer/id #uuid "e80a29ed-94a2-591f-9412-59e8778e3657" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4ca3c1c9-556c-5824-8f78-bd156ab7ec4c" :answer/id #uuid "4ca3c1c9-556c-5824-8f78-bd156ab7ec4c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-940a3ea8-8fb7-5661-9e30-87d5a6990359" :answer/id #uuid "940a3ea8-8fb7-5661-9e30-87d5a6990359" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6291fa02-aa1b-5b93-a9bb-731989a0e370" :answer/id #uuid "6291fa02-aa1b-5b93-a9bb-731989a0e370" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-23762144-a04a-50eb-ae41-7d91386b34ba" :answer/id #uuid "23762144-a04a-50eb-ae41-7d91386b34ba" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III and IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-2a221524-8030-56ce-9632-c32e19b2687d" :question/id #uuid "2a221524-8030-56ce-9632-c32e19b2687d" :question/math-academy-id "q-333341" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true when testing the stability of Euler's method on the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),\\,y(0)=4,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda =2,$ and step size $Δx?$  \n\nI. The update formula can be written as ${y}_{n+1}=(1-2Δx)^{n}{y}_{n}.$\n\nII. The method is stable for $|1-2Δx|<1.$\n\nIII. The method is stable for step size $Δx=0.2.$\n\nIV. The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx=0.9.$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method ${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$ is stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=4,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of Euler's Method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-2y,\\,y(0)=4.\n$$\n\nFirst, for an initial value problem ${y}^{′}=f(x,y),y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},$ Euler's method is given by the update formula\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx.\n$$\n\nNow, let's check our statements:\n\n- Applying Euler's method to the test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+(-2{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}-2{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(1-2Δx){y}_{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, statement I is false.\n\n- For stability, we require ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty .$ Here, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n} & =(1-2Δx){y}_{n-1} \\\\  & =(1-2Δx)((1-2Δx){y}_{n-2}) \\\\  & =(1-2Δx)^{2}{y}_{n-2} \\\\  & \\,\\,⋮\\,\\,⋮ \\\\  & =(1-2Δx)^{n}{y}_{0} \\\\  & =4(1-2Δx)^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${y}_{n}$ converges to $0$ as $n\\to \\infty $ if and only if $|1-2Δx|<1,$ as stated. Hence, statement II is true.\n\n- Expanding the inequality $|1-2Δx|<1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & <1-2Δx<1 \\\\ -2 & <-2Δx<0 \\\\ 0 & <2Δx<2 \\\\ 0 & <Δx<1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine stability for the given step sizes:\n\n- Since $Δx=0.2$ satisfies the above inequality, Euler's method is stable for this value. Hence, statement III is true.\n\n- Since $Δx=0.9$ also satisfies the above inequality, Euler's method is stable for this value. Hence, statement IV is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-11f4efad-b4bc-5bca-b55b-4507e7643203"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-11f4efad-b4bc-5bca-b55b-4507e7643203" :answer-field/id #uuid "11f4efad-b4bc-5bca-b55b-4507e7643203" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-387be892-c63c-59ae-a09d-74052a3a2927" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f434e6a6-90ca-5749-aef2-568d3146475f" "answer-cfff59b2-4866-5004-8fdc-3521531421f7" "answer-387be892-c63c-59ae-a09d-74052a3a2927" "answer-4296a04e-f6b1-5aaf-85c5-19b349421dd3" "answer-827dbed1-a595-5bf3-948b-881cba5d2322"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f434e6a6-90ca-5749-aef2-568d3146475f" :answer/id #uuid "f434e6a6-90ca-5749-aef2-568d3146475f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cfff59b2-4866-5004-8fdc-3521531421f7" :answer/id #uuid "cfff59b2-4866-5004-8fdc-3521531421f7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-387be892-c63c-59ae-a09d-74052a3a2927" :answer/id #uuid "387be892-c63c-59ae-a09d-74052a3a2927" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4296a04e-f6b1-5aaf-85c5-19b349421dd3" :answer/id #uuid "4296a04e-f6b1-5aaf-85c5-19b349421dd3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II, III, and IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-827dbed1-a595-5bf3-948b-881cba5d2322" :answer/id #uuid "827dbed1-a595-5bf3-948b-881cba5d2322" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III and IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186308441 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true when testing the stability of Euler's method on the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),\\,y(0)=3,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda =\\frac{7}{3},$ and step size $Δx?$  \n\nI. The update formula can be written as ${y}_{n+1}={(1-\\frac{7}{3}Δx)}^{n}{y}_{n}.$\n\nII. The method is stable for $|1-\\frac{7}{3}Δx|>1.$\n\nIII. The method is stable for step size $Δx=0.2.$\n\nIV. The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx=0.9.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-1f49a9a1-5ba0-5f85-aec8-d5a800fd601f" :question/id #uuid "1f49a9a1-5ba0-5f85-aec8-d5a800fd601f" :question/math-academy-id "q-334159" :question/problem "For which of the following step sizes is Euler's Method **unstable**, given that $\\lambda =18$ is the parameter of the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=1,x\\ge 0,\\lambda >0,$ and step size $Δx?$\n\nI. $Δx=0.04$\n\nII. $Δx=0.1$\n\nIII. $Δx=0.15$\n\nIV. $Δx=0.2$\n\n*Hint: The stability function for Euler's method is given by* $R(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx.$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nFor stability, we require that the following condition holds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(\\lambda Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |1-\\lambda Δx| & <1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this gives\n\n$$\n0<\\lambda Δx<2.\n$$\n\nThis means that Euler’s method is stable when applied to the test equation for values in the region\n$0<\\lambda Δx<2.$\n\nIn our case, where $\\lambda =18,$ this region simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & <18Δx<2 \\\\ 0 & <Δx<\\frac{2}{18} \\\\ 0 & <Δx<0.111….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, of the given step sizes:\n\n- $Δx=0.04$ *satisfies* the inequality,\n\n- $Δx=0.1$ *satisfies* the inequality,\n\n- $Δx=0.15$ *doesn't satisfy* the inequality, and\n\n- $Δx=0.2$ *doesn't satisfy* the inequality.\n\nHence, Euler’s method is unstable for the step sizes $Δx=0.15$ and $Δx=0.2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III and IV only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a48d3581-9e1a-5b6e-a7e4-0c504bb36bea"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a48d3581-9e1a-5b6e-a7e4-0c504bb36bea" :answer-field/id #uuid "a48d3581-9e1a-5b6e-a7e4-0c504bb36bea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e072a38f-d476-562e-a891-5ff2765fe248" :answer-field/choices ["answer-65993759-bea5-5e08-9cbd-c015ba48062e" "answer-a31a3454-2778-5b31-a87b-5ee7235dff3a" "answer-d20a241d-f4b6-5454-901b-02b9a0eb489a" "answer-e072a38f-d476-562e-a891-5ff2765fe248" "answer-1ca48805-a4cc-57b6-a02a-f3dd2f06b6f3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-65993759-bea5-5e08-9cbd-c015ba48062e" :answer/id #uuid "65993759-bea5-5e08-9cbd-c015ba48062e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a31a3454-2778-5b31-a87b-5ee7235dff3a" :answer/id #uuid "a31a3454-2778-5b31-a87b-5ee7235dff3a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, III, and IV" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d20a241d-f4b6-5454-901b-02b9a0eb489a" :answer/id #uuid "d20a241d-f4b6-5454-901b-02b9a0eb489a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II, III, and IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e072a38f-d476-562e-a891-5ff2765fe248" :answer/id #uuid "e072a38f-d476-562e-a891-5ff2765fe248" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III and IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1ca48805-a4cc-57b6-a02a-f3dd2f06b6f3" :answer/id #uuid "1ca48805-a4cc-57b6-a02a-f3dd2f06b6f3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-eb08f354-4c44-52a2-9c76-c85c4970a5f5" :question/id #uuid "eb08f354-4c44-52a2-9c76-c85c4970a5f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-334160" :question/problem "For which of the following step sizes is Euler's Method stable, given that $\\lambda =5$ is the parameter of the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=1,x\\ge 0,$ and step size $Δx?$\n\nI. $Δx=0.1$\n\nII. $Δx=0.25$\n\nIII. $Δx=0.3$\n\nIV. $Δx=1.5$\n\n*Hint: The stability function for Euler's method is given by* $R(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx.$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method ${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$is stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nFor stability, we require that the following condition holds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(\\lambda Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |1-\\lambda Δx| & <1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this gives\n\n$$\n0<\\lambda Δx<2.\n$$\n\nThis means that Euler's method is stable when applied to the test equation for values in the region $0<\\lambda Δx<2.$\n\nIn our case, where $\\lambda =5,$ this region simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & <5Δx<2 \\\\ 0 & <Δx<\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ 0 & <Δx<0.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, of the given step sizes:\n\n- $Δx=0.1$ satisfies the inequality,\n\n- $Δx=0.25$ satisfies the inequality,\n\n- $Δx=0.3$ satisfies the inequality, and\n\n- $Δx=1.5$ doesn't satisfy the inequality.\n\nTherefore, Euler’s method is stable for step sizes $Δx=0.1,$ $Δx=0.25,$ and $Δx=0.3.$\n\nThus, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f7768791-4d77-54d9-9010-4c03c60e2cb4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f7768791-4d77-54d9-9010-4c03c60e2cb4" :answer-field/id #uuid "f7768791-4d77-54d9-9010-4c03c60e2cb4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-36a41ac0-3dcb-5465-bbc8-e436baf0c781" :answer-field/choices ["answer-cfa1ac7e-ac77-5086-bdb8-5025172a2118" "answer-17b2b535-ce9b-5bd8-8330-c13adc2e4434" "answer-36a41ac0-3dcb-5465-bbc8-e436baf0c781" "answer-17f618d4-efd6-526f-bfc1-cf43d50299b8" "answer-00e8c9d1-6c3b-5937-901e-1e613b7c928a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cfa1ac7e-ac77-5086-bdb8-5025172a2118" :answer/id #uuid "cfa1ac7e-ac77-5086-bdb8-5025172a2118" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-17b2b535-ce9b-5bd8-8330-c13adc2e4434" :answer/id #uuid "17b2b535-ce9b-5bd8-8330-c13adc2e4434" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, III, and IV" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-36a41ac0-3dcb-5465-bbc8-e436baf0c781" :answer/id #uuid "36a41ac0-3dcb-5465-bbc8-e436baf0c781" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-17f618d4-efd6-526f-bfc1-cf43d50299b8" :answer/id #uuid "17f618d4-efd6-526f-bfc1-cf43d50299b8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-00e8c9d1-6c3b-5937-901e-1e613b7c928a" :answer/id #uuid "00e8c9d1-6c3b-5937-901e-1e613b7c928a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186308542 :question/problem "For which of the following step sizes is Euler's Method **stable**, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=1,x\\ge 0,\\lambda >0,$ and step size $Δx?$\n\nI. $Δx=\\frac{1.4}{\\lambda }$\n\nII. $Δx=\\frac{1.25}{\\lambda }$\n\nIII. $Δx=\\frac{2.5}{\\lambda }$\n\nIV. $Δx=\\frac{0.75}{\\lambda }$\n\n*Hint: The stability function for Euler's method is given by* $R(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186372670 :question/problem "For which of the following values is Euler's method **unstable**, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=1,x\\ge 0,\\lambda >0,$ and step size $Δx?$  \n\nI. $\\lambda Δx=0.5$\n\nII. $\\lambda Δx=1.8$\n\nIII. $\\lambda Δx=2.1$\n\nIV. $\\lambda Δx=3$\n\n*Hint: The stability function for Euler's method is given by* $R(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-28574ce8-fd0a-501f-b680-ed91403e93fc" :question/id #uuid "28574ce8-fd0a-501f-b680-ed91403e93fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-334229" :question/problem "For which of the following values is Euler's method **unstable**, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=1,x\\ge 0,\\lambda >0,$ and step size $Δx?$\n\nI. $\\lambda Δx=1.2$\n\nII. $\\lambda Δx=2.4$\n\nIII. $\\lambda Δx=0.9$\n\nIV. $\\lambda Δx=2.8$\n\n*Hint: The stability function for Euler's method is given by* $R(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx.$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nFor stability, we require that the following condition holds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(\\lambda Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |1-\\lambda Δx| & <1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this gives\n\n$$\n0<\\lambda Δx<2.\n$$\n\nThis means that Euler's method is stable when applied to the test equation for values in the region $0<\\lambda Δx<2.$ Of the given values:\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=1.2,$ falls *within* the given region,\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=2.4,$ falls *outside* the given region.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=0.9,$ falls *within* the given region,\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=2.8,$ falls *outside* the given region.\n\nHence, Euler's method is **unstable** for the values $\\lambda Δx=2.4,$ and $\\lambda Δx=2.8.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and IV only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-731062da-df90-5f98-9744-a1d577715f5e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-731062da-df90-5f98-9744-a1d577715f5e" :answer-field/id #uuid "731062da-df90-5f98-9744-a1d577715f5e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-2ad44cc1-1a30-587d-972c-8d1aa29786fe" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2f8a5102-d9ce-5281-8226-f6e1f88e351e" "answer-c1028ea5-b3fe-5c8f-b032-bf7029fb51fc" "answer-2ad44cc1-1a30-587d-972c-8d1aa29786fe" "answer-d857e2c0-26d6-5954-a9f6-413d905b0f18" "answer-9d5e4941-d25d-5a92-9dc2-f18394b45f95"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2f8a5102-d9ce-5281-8226-f6e1f88e351e" :answer/id #uuid "2f8a5102-d9ce-5281-8226-f6e1f88e351e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, III, and IV" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c1028ea5-b3fe-5c8f-b032-bf7029fb51fc" :answer/id #uuid "c1028ea5-b3fe-5c8f-b032-bf7029fb51fc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2ad44cc1-1a30-587d-972c-8d1aa29786fe" :answer/id #uuid "2ad44cc1-1a30-587d-972c-8d1aa29786fe" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d857e2c0-26d6-5954-a9f6-413d905b0f18" :answer/id #uuid "d857e2c0-26d6-5954-a9f6-413d905b0f18" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9d5e4941-d25d-5a92-9dc2-f18394b45f95" :answer/id #uuid "9d5e4941-d25d-5a92-9dc2-f18394b45f95" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-acf9ba03-3b41-5ee5-9008-523784ef9cff" :question/id #uuid "acf9ba03-3b41-5ee5-9008-523784ef9cff" :question/math-academy-id "q-332551" :question/problem "For the initial value problem ${y}^{′}=f(x,y),y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},$ the modified Euler method can be expressed as the update formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{n}^{(p)} & =f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ {y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}^{(p)}))\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true when testing the stability of the modified Euler method on the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=1,x\\ge 0,\\lambda =5,$ and step size $Δx?$\n\nI. The stability function is $R(5Δx)=1-5Δx+\\frac{25}{2}(Δx)^{2}.$\n\nII. The stability region is $|\\frac{25}{2}{(Δx-\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}|<1.$\n\nIII. The method is absolutely stable.\n\nIV. The method is stable for step size $Δx=0.5.$\n\n*Hint:* The stability function $R$ is given by ${y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n}.$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nA method that is stable for all possible values $\\lambda Δx>0$ is called absolutely stable.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of the modified Euler Method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-5y,\\,y(0)=1.\n$$\n\nWe now examine our statements:\n\n- Applying the modified Euler method to this test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}^{(p)} & ={y}_{n}+f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}-5{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n}-5{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx))\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(-5{y}_{n}-5({y}_{n}-5{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx))\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(-10{y}_{n}+25{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx)\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}-5{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx+\\frac{25}{2}{y}_{n}\\cdot (Δx)^{2} \\\\  & =[1-5Δx+\\frac{25}{2}(Δx)^{2}]{y}_{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the stability function is $R(5Δx)=1-5Δx+\\frac{25}{2}(Δx)^{2}.$ Hence, statement I is true.\n\n- The method is stable when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(5Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |1-5Δx+\\frac{25}{2}(Δx)^{2}| & <1 \\\\ |\\frac{25}{2}{(Δx-\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}| & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${y}_{n}$ converges to $0$ as $n\\to \\infty $ if and only if\n\n$$\n|\\frac{25}{2}{(Δx-\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}|<1.\n$$\n\nHence, statement II is false.\n\n- Now, since the argument of the absolute value is always non-negative, we can remove the absolute value. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{25}{2}{(Δx-\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2} & <1 \\\\ \\frac{25}{2}{(Δx-\\frac{1}{5})}^{2} & <\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {(Δx-\\frac{1}{5})}^{2} & <\\frac{1}{25} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{5}<Δx-\\frac{1}{5} & <\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ 0<Δx & <\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the modified Euler method is not absolutely stable, and statement III is false.\n\n- Finally, since it does not satisfy this inequality, the modified Euler method is unstable for step size $Δx=0.5,$ and hence statement IV is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a20e667f-eaef-54c2-a630-c9608612d08e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a20e667f-eaef-54c2-a630-c9608612d08e" :answer-field/id #uuid "a20e667f-eaef-54c2-a630-c9608612d08e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-4ea03876-e7b7-599e-9ef0-7b3496b85843" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3556d6b8-e7e7-52ee-a721-309c17d86bf0" "answer-4ea03876-e7b7-599e-9ef0-7b3496b85843" "answer-0f85c490-3e69-52b0-8ce5-3cf73154d5e5" "answer-30ff0ac2-1851-5a0f-994d-3b7117c94537" "answer-e8d1bd98-986b-52aa-ae5e-028afa43b9a7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3556d6b8-e7e7-52ee-a721-309c17d86bf0" :answer/id #uuid "3556d6b8-e7e7-52ee-a721-309c17d86bf0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4ea03876-e7b7-599e-9ef0-7b3496b85843" :answer/id #uuid "4ea03876-e7b7-599e-9ef0-7b3496b85843" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0f85c490-3e69-52b0-8ce5-3cf73154d5e5" :answer/id #uuid "0f85c490-3e69-52b0-8ce5-3cf73154d5e5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, III, and IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-30ff0ac2-1851-5a0f-994d-3b7117c94537" :answer/id #uuid "30ff0ac2-1851-5a0f-994d-3b7117c94537" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e8d1bd98-986b-52aa-ae5e-028afa43b9a7" :answer/id #uuid "e8d1bd98-986b-52aa-ae5e-028afa43b9a7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II, III, and IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-639c33be-3d9f-5ed3-823e-9a34ba608c41" :question/id #uuid "639c33be-3d9f-5ed3-823e-9a34ba608c41" :question/math-academy-id "q-333543" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true when testing the stability of the implicit Euler method on the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=1,x\\ge 0,\\lambda =2,$ and step size $Δx?$  \n\nI. The stability function is $R(2Δx)=\\frac{1}{1-2Δx}.$\n\nII. The stability region is $|\\frac{1}{1-2Δx}|<1.$\n\nIII. The method is absolutely stable.\n\nIV. The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx=0.30.$\n\n*Hint:* The stability function $R$ is given by ${y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n}.$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method \n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nA method that is stable for all possible values $\\lambda Δx>0$ is called absolutely stable.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of the implicit Euler method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-2y,\\,y(0)=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, for an initial value problem ${y}^{′}=f(x,y),y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},$ the implicit Euler method is given by the update formula\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n+1})\\cdot Δx.\n$$\n\nWe now examine our statements:\n\n- Applying the implicit Euler method to the test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+(-2{y}_{n+1})\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{n+1}+2{y}_{n+1}\\cdot Δx & ={y}_{n} \\\\ {y}_{n+1}(1+2Δx) & ={y}_{n} \\\\ {y}_{n+1} & =\\frac{1}{1+2Δx}\\cdot {y}_{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the stability function is \n\n$$\nR(2Δx)=\\frac{1}{1+2Δx}.\n$$\n\nHence, statement I is false.\n\n- The method is stable when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(2Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |\\frac{1}{1+2Δx}| & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${y}_{n}$ converges to $0$ as $n\\to \\infty $ if and only if\n$|\\frac{1}{1+2Δx}|<1.$\nHence, statement II is false.\n\n- Now, since $1+2Δx>1$ for any positive step size $Δx,$ we have\n\n$$\n|\\frac{1}{1+2Δx}|<1\n$$\n\nfor all $Δx>0.$ So, the implicit Euler method is absolutely stable, and statement III is true.\n\n- Finally, because the implicit Euler method is absolutely stable, it is stable for step size $Δx=0.30.$ Hence, statement IV is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"III only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-99313e35-024c-59e4-a5fd-2eb3a772c5b4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-99313e35-024c-59e4-a5fd-2eb3a772c5b4" :answer-field/id #uuid "99313e35-024c-59e4-a5fd-2eb3a772c5b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f302fc2f-4306-5237-8472-717492ad0535" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8e7f7dbc-4ab3-5c1e-946c-e0e613a99dab" "answer-e0c4ec02-82b2-539f-9253-114598fb8d13" "answer-a6f7454c-5788-50a5-91e8-6822fa168488" "answer-c2937865-c1bb-56d7-89e1-547031b23e84" "answer-f302fc2f-4306-5237-8472-717492ad0535"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8e7f7dbc-4ab3-5c1e-946c-e0e613a99dab" :answer/id #uuid "8e7f7dbc-4ab3-5c1e-946c-e0e613a99dab" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e0c4ec02-82b2-539f-9253-114598fb8d13" :answer/id #uuid "e0c4ec02-82b2-539f-9253-114598fb8d13" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a6f7454c-5788-50a5-91e8-6822fa168488" :answer/id #uuid "a6f7454c-5788-50a5-91e8-6822fa168488" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c2937865-c1bb-56d7-89e1-547031b23e84" :answer/id #uuid "c2937865-c1bb-56d7-89e1-547031b23e84" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f302fc2f-4306-5237-8472-717492ad0535" :answer/id #uuid "f302fc2f-4306-5237-8472-717492ad0535" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-ad84f498-bd85-5632-acda-fbc8df689a1e" :question/id #uuid "ad84f498-bd85-5632-acda-fbc8df689a1e" :question/math-academy-id "q-333983" :question/problem "For the initial value problem ${y}^{′}=f(x,y),y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},$ the modified Euler method can be expressed as the update formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{n}^{(p)} & =f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ {y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}^{(p)}))\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true when testing the stability of the modified Euler method on the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=1,x\\ge 0,\\lambda =8,$ and step size $Δx?$  \n\nI. The stability function is $R(8Δx)=1-8Δx+32(Δx)^{2}.$\n\nII. The stability region is $|32{(Δx-\\frac{1}{8})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}|<1.$\n\nIII. The method is absolutely stable.\n\nIV. The method is stable for step size $Δx=0.6.$\n\n*Hint:* The stability function $R$ is given by ${y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n}.$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method ${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$ is stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nA method that is stable for all possible values $\\lambda Δx>0$ is called absolutely stable.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of the modified Euler Method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-8y,\\,y(0)=1.\n$$\n\nWe now examine our statements:\n\n- Applying the modified Euler method to this test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}^{(p)} & ={y}_{n}+f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}-8{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n}-8{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx))\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(-8{y}_{n}-8({y}_{n}-8{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx))\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(-16{y}_{n}+64{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx)\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}-8{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx+32{y}_{n}\\cdot (Δx)^{2} \\\\  & =[1-8Δx+32(Δx)^{2}]{y}_{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the stability function is $R(8Δx)=1-8Δx+32(Δx)^{2}.$ Hence, statement I is true.\n\n- The method is stable when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(8Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |1-8Δx+32(Δx)^{2}| & <1 \\\\ |32{(Δx-\\frac{1}{8})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}| & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${y}_{n}$ converges to $0$ as $n\\to \\infty $ if and only if\n\n$$\n|32{(Δx-\\frac{1}{8})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}|<1.\n$$\n\nHence, statement II is true.\n\n- Now, since the argument of the absolute value is always non-negative, we can remove the absolute value. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}32{(Δx-\\frac{1}{8})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2} & <1 \\\\ 32{(Δx-\\frac{1}{8})}^{2} & <\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {(Δx-\\frac{1}{8})}^{2} & <\\frac{1}{64} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{8}<Δx-\\frac{1}{8} & <\\frac{1}{8} \\\\ 0<Δx & <\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the modified Euler method is not absolutely stable, and statement III is false.\n\n- Finally, it does *not* satisfy this inequality, the modified Euler method is unstable for step size $Δx=0.6,$ and hence statement IV is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-fd178739-0906-50de-a480-2154dbc44ed0"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-fd178739-0906-50de-a480-2154dbc44ed0" :answer-field/id #uuid "fd178739-0906-50de-a480-2154dbc44ed0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-317432c0-fd64-5125-98ee-c47f3bf55647" :answer-field/choices ["answer-317432c0-fd64-5125-98ee-c47f3bf55647" "answer-43d767f0-e8f9-5674-9dde-916febb3c307" "answer-fa20b4e5-82b7-594c-94a0-7601e9d68d6f" "answer-17e4957c-db24-5341-832a-43c407aa8994" "answer-0ebb3711-fb02-5497-9e8b-b7169c43e885"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-317432c0-fd64-5125-98ee-c47f3bf55647" :answer/id #uuid "317432c0-fd64-5125-98ee-c47f3bf55647" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-43d767f0-e8f9-5674-9dde-916febb3c307" :answer/id #uuid "43d767f0-e8f9-5674-9dde-916febb3c307" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fa20b4e5-82b7-594c-94a0-7601e9d68d6f" :answer/id #uuid "fa20b4e5-82b7-594c-94a0-7601e9d68d6f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, III, and IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-17e4957c-db24-5341-832a-43c407aa8994" :answer/id #uuid "17e4957c-db24-5341-832a-43c407aa8994" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0ebb3711-fb02-5497-9e8b-b7169c43e885" :answer/id #uuid "0ebb3711-fb02-5497-9e8b-b7169c43e885" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II, III, and IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186372641 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true when testing the stability of the implicit Euler method on the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=1,x\\ge 0,\\lambda =1,$ and step size $Δx?$  \n\nI. The stability function is $R(Δx)=\\frac{1}{1+Δx}.$\n\nII. The stability region is $|\\frac{1}{1+Δx}|<1.$\n\nIII. The method is absolutely stable.\n\nIV. The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx=0.50.$\n\n*Hint:* The stability function $R$ is given by ${y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186308461 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true when testing the stability of the implicit Euler method on the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=1,x\\ge 0,\\lambda =6,$ and step size $Δx?$  \n\nI. The stability function is $R(6Δx)=\\frac{1}{1-6Δx}.$\n\nII. The stability region is $|\\frac{1}{1+6Δx}|<1.$\n\nIII. The method is absolutely stable.\n\nIV. The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx=0.10.$\n\n*Hint:* The stability function $R$ is given by ${y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186229645 :question/problem "For which of the following values is the implicit Euler method stable, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation?\n\nI. $\\lambda Δx=0.25$\n\nII. $\\lambda Δx=1$\n\nIII. $\\lambda Δx=1.5$\n\nIV. $\\lambda Δx=9$\n\n*Hint: The stability function $R$ of the implicit Euler method is given by*\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δ)=\\frac{1}{1+\\lambda Δx}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-b1d62ba0-0114-5483-9f12-c0fecd4e79bf" :question/id #uuid "b1d62ba0-0114-5483-9f12-c0fecd4e79bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-334467" :question/problem "For which of the following values is the Trapezoidal Method stable, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation?\n\nI. $\\lambda Δx=0.45$\n\nII. $\\lambda Δx=1.7$\n\nIII. $\\lambda Δx=3.2$\n\nIV. $\\lambda Δx=9.4$\n\n*Hint: The stability function $R$ of the Trapezoidal Method is given by*\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=\\frac{1-\\lambda Δx/2}{\\,1+\\lambda Δx/2}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method  ${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$ is stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=5,\\,x\\ge 0,\\text{ }\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx)\\,{y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is referred to as the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nThe stability function of the trapezoidal method is\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=\\frac{1-\\lambda Δx/2}{1+\\lambda Δx/2}.\n$$\n\nLet's check the stability of the trapezoidal method for each of the given values in turn.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=0.45,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(0.45)| & =|\\frac{1-0.45/2}{1+0.45/2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{0.775}{1.225}| \\\\  & =0.626…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls within the stability region, the trapezoidal method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=1.7,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(1.7)| & =|\\frac{1-1.7/2}{1+1.7/2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{0.15}{1.85}| \\\\  & =0.0810…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls within the stability region, the trapezoidal method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=3.2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(3.2)| & =|\\frac{1-3.2/2}{1+3.2/2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{-0.6}{2.6}| \\\\  & =0.2307…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls within the stability region, the trapezoidal method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=9.4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(9.4)| & =|\\frac{1-9.4/2}{1+9.4/2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{-3.7}{5.7}| \\\\  & =0.6491…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls within the stability region, the trapezoidal method is stable.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “I, II, III, and IV”.\n\nNote that, since the trapezoidal method is absolutely stable, it is stable for all possible values of $\\lambda Δx.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5a3dee3b-6477-52a6-9c3e-9c596208389a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5a3dee3b-6477-52a6-9c3e-9c596208389a" :answer-field/id #uuid "5a3dee3b-6477-52a6-9c3e-9c596208389a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-cefcee36-215c-5ac8-85f4-de7dc59960e9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d6cb56c6-fca0-5868-9d2a-5d8c10774960" "answer-cefcee36-215c-5ac8-85f4-de7dc59960e9" "answer-726a8916-f18c-50cf-b900-0018c5555506" "answer-b3b4bea1-352b-5c6e-9d27-5203877f2b72" "answer-df768aea-08f8-5386-bc64-62485a777115"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d6cb56c6-fca0-5868-9d2a-5d8c10774960" :answer/id #uuid "d6cb56c6-fca0-5868-9d2a-5d8c10774960" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cefcee36-215c-5ac8-85f4-de7dc59960e9" :answer/id #uuid "cefcee36-215c-5ac8-85f4-de7dc59960e9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, III, and IV" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-726a8916-f18c-50cf-b900-0018c5555506" :answer/id #uuid "726a8916-f18c-50cf-b900-0018c5555506" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, III, and IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b3b4bea1-352b-5c6e-9d27-5203877f2b72" :answer/id #uuid "b3b4bea1-352b-5c6e-9d27-5203877f2b72" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-df768aea-08f8-5386-bc64-62485a777115" :answer/id #uuid "df768aea-08f8-5386-bc64-62485a777115" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186308585 :question/problem "For which of the following values is the implicit Euler method stable, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation?\n\nI. $\\lambda Δx=0.95$\n\nII. $\\lambda Δx=1.2$\n\nIII. $\\lambda Δx=0.4$\n\nIV. $\\lambda Δx=2.3$\n\n*Hint: The stability function $R$ of the implicit Euler method is given by*\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=\\frac{1}{1+\\lambda Δx}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-cf3c4ec0-335d-5e9d-91d7-9dd1f9e287ee" :question/id #uuid "cf3c4ec0-335d-5e9d-91d7-9dd1f9e287ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-334716" :question/problem "For which of the following values is the modified Euler method stable, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation?\n\nI. $\\lambda Δx=0.8$\n\nII. $\\lambda Δx=1.2$\n\nIII. $\\lambda Δx=1.9$\n\nIV. $\\lambda Δx=2.6$\n\n*Hint: The stability function $R$ of the modified Euler method is given by*\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{2}}{2}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is referred to as the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nThe stability function of the modified Euler method is\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's check the stability of the modified Euler method for each of the given values in turn.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=0.8,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(0.8)| & =|1-0.8+\\frac{(0.8)^{2}}{2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{13}{25}| \\\\  & =0.52<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the modified Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=1.2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(1.2)| & =|1-1.2+\\frac{(1.2)^{2}}{2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{13}{25}| \\\\  & =0.52<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the modified Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=1.9,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(1.9)| & =|1-1.9+\\frac{(1.9)^{2}}{2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{181}{200}| \\\\  & =0.905<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the modified Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=2.6,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(2.6)| & =|1-2.6+\\frac{(2.6)^{2}}{2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{89}{50}| \\\\  & =1.78>1.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *outside* the stability region, the modified Euler method is unstable.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only\".\n\nNote that the stability region of the modified Euler method is $0<\\lambda Δx<2,$ so it is not absolutely stable." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1e125c54-8037-50e8-adb8-e09c42c57dcb"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1e125c54-8037-50e8-adb8-e09c42c57dcb" :answer-field/id #uuid "1e125c54-8037-50e8-adb8-e09c42c57dcb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-da9e1407-b76c-5d92-bb7a-5f29f8e66925" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d7d1a302-96ae-51b0-b8b5-4844e48c6d5f" "answer-a09dcd1b-7350-52e3-9bc1-b68407937613" "answer-da9e1407-b76c-5d92-bb7a-5f29f8e66925" "answer-e55eb4e7-7754-5ff5-9b01-0d7884883a8d" "answer-ad7c35d1-bbc7-51f9-bdea-4c81575cd295"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d7d1a302-96ae-51b0-b8b5-4844e48c6d5f" :answer/id #uuid "d7d1a302-96ae-51b0-b8b5-4844e48c6d5f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a09dcd1b-7350-52e3-9bc1-b68407937613" :answer/id #uuid "a09dcd1b-7350-52e3-9bc1-b68407937613" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, III, and IV" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-da9e1407-b76c-5d92-bb7a-5f29f8e66925" :answer/id #uuid "da9e1407-b76c-5d92-bb7a-5f29f8e66925" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e55eb4e7-7754-5ff5-9b01-0d7884883a8d" :answer/id #uuid "e55eb4e7-7754-5ff5-9b01-0d7884883a8d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ad7c35d1-bbc7-51f9-bdea-4c81575cd295" :answer/id #uuid "ad7c35d1-bbc7-51f9-bdea-4c81575cd295" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186308624 :question/problem "For which of the following values is the trapezoidal method stable, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation?\n\nI. $\\lambda Δx=0.15$\n\nII. $\\lambda Δx=0.8$\n\nIII. $\\lambda Δx=2.9$\n\nIV. $\\lambda Δx=6.2$\n\n*Hint: The stability function $R$ of the trapezoidal method is given by*\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=\\frac{1-\\lambda Δx/2}{1+\\lambda Δx/2}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186230185 :tutorial/content "Now, let's check the stability of the implicit Euler method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\ny' = -\\lambda y, \\qquad y(0) = 1, \\quad x \\geq 0, \\quad \\lambda > 0.\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that, for an initial value problem $y' = f(x,y), y(x_0) = y_0,$ the implicit Euler method is given by the update formula\n\n$$\ny_{n+1} = y_n + f(x_{n+1},y_{n+1}) \\cdot \\Delta x.\n$$\n\nApplying the implicit Euler method to the test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+(-\\lambda {y}_{n+1})\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{n+1}+\\lambda {y}_{n+1}\\cdot Δx & ={y}_{n} \\\\ {y}_{n+1}(1+\\lambda Δx) & ={y}_{n} \\\\ {y}_{n+1} & =\\frac{1}{1+\\lambda Δx}\\cdot {y}_{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the stability function is \n\n$$\nR(\\lambda\\Delta x) = \\dfrac{1}{1 + \\lambda\\Delta x}.\n$$\n\nThe method is stable when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(\\lambda Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |\\frac{1}{1+\\lambda Δx}| & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y_n$ converges to $0$ as $n \\to \\infty$ if and only if\n$\\left|\\dfrac{1}{1 + \\lambda\\Delta x}\\right| < 1.$\n\nSince $1 + \\lambda\\Delta x > 1$ for any positive step size $\\Delta x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\left|\\dfrac{1}{1 + \\lambda\\Delta x}\\right| < 1\n$$\n\nfor all $\\Delta x > 0.$ So, the implicit Euler method is absolutely stable!\n\nThe diagram below shows the exact solution curve with the numerical steps of both the explicit and the implicit Euler methods for the value $\\lambda\\Delta x = 2.$ \nWe saw before that the explicit method oscillates between the values $-1$ and $1$ and does not converge to $0.$\n\nConversely, the numerical steps of the implicit Euler method converge to $0$ quite quickly for the same step size!\n\n![](images/08/083b5c0193a2a35648434cae4dcf1820fd47c86202c13a7e7e2e078d45d52bdb.png)\n\nImplicit methods tend to be more numerically stable than explicit methods." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186229253 :activity/expected-seconds 1500.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
